<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Faxian</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 399–414</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Faxian was a Chinese Buddhist monk who travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in search of Buddhist texts, a journey that lasted from 399 to 412, about ten years of it in India. His account of the journey describes the prosperity, peace and mild government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-ii">Chandragupta II</a>, making it a valuable outside source for the period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/faxian.jpg" alt="Faxian" width="266" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ms Sarah Welch · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Faxian_statue_in_Maritime_Experiential_Museum_%26_Aquarium_Singapore.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The pilgrim <b>Faxian</b>, who set out from the north in 399, returned by sea in 412 and later translated texts at Jiankang, while Huiyuan's treatise arguing that monks need not bow to rulers framed a lasting question in Chinese politics.</blockquote><a href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Chinese pilgrim <b>Faxian</b>, who travelled in India in the first decade of the fifth century, described light taxation, mild punishments, and effective administration, an account broadly consistent with other evidence of prosperity in the Gangetic heartland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Chinese pilgrim <b>Faxian</b>, in India around 400 under Chandragupta II, reported that the king ruled without executions: most criminals were only fined, and repeated rebels lost their right hand.</blockquote><a href="/societies/classical-india">Social Hierarchy of Classical India</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Xiangyuan County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.53,113.04&amp;year=406" aria-label="Xiangyuan County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7391 846 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Faxian"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7841 1116h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7841 1116h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.53,113.04&amp;year=406">What was here in 406 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Gazing on all sides, as far as the eye can reach, in order to mark the track, it would be impossible to succeed but for the rotting bones of dead men which point the way.&#8221;</p><footer>A Record of Buddhistic Kingdoms: the desert of Lop (in Giles, A History of Chinese Literature) &#183; tr. Herbert A. Giles, 1901 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/43711/pg43711-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The temperature is very agreeable in this country; there is no distinction of summer and winter. The trees and plants are always green, and cultivation of the soil is carried on as men please, without regard to seasons.&#8221;</p><footer>A Record of Buddhistic Kingdoms: Ceylon (in Giles, A History of Chinese Literature) &#183; tr. Herbert A. Giles, 1901 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/43711/pg43711-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I have journeyed afar in search of the Law. Oh, that by your awful power you would turn back the flow of the leak that we might reach some resting-place!&#8221;</p><footer>A Record of Buddhistic Kingdoms: a storm at sea, 413 (in Giles, Gems of Chinese Literature) &#183; tr. Herbert A. Giles, 1923 &#183; his prayer as the ship sprang a leak &#183; <a href="https://gutenberg.net.au/ebooks08/0800161.txt" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg Australia</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;When I look back on what I have gone through, my heart is involuntarily moved, and the perspiration flows forth. That I encountered danger and trod the most perilous places, without thinking of or sparing myself, was because I had a definite aim…&#8221;</p><footer>A Record of Buddhistic Kingdoms, closing note &#183; tr. James Legge, 1886 &#183; his words as a monk who met him in 416 wrote them down &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2124/pg2124-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I did not quit the family in compliance with my father’s wishes, but because I wished to be far from the dust and vulgar ways of life. This is why I chose monkhood.&#8221;</p><footer>His reply as a boy to an uncle who urged him to go home to his mother (Memoirs of Eminent Monks, 519, in Legge's introduction) &#183; tr. James Legge, 1886 &#183; as the Memoirs of Eminent Monks record it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2124/pg2124-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…it was your former neglect of charity which brought you to your present state of destitution; and now, again, you wish to rob others.&#8221;</p><footer>To thieves who came to take the monks' rice (Memoirs of Eminent Monks, 519, in Legge's introduction) &#183; tr. James Legge, 1886 &#183; as the Memoirs of Eminent Monks record it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2124/pg2124-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
